           A commitment is an action taken in the present that binds an organization to
      a future course of action. A decision to act as a first mover is usually binding and
      should not be taken lightly. Preemptive actions can deter potential rivals from
      entering but may also result in heavy, irreversible investments [Sull, 2005].
           The entrepreneur considers entering a market during an estimated period of
      opportunity often called a window of opportunity. The first mover envisions a
      greater cash flow as a result of early entry, as shown in Figure 5.1. Uncertainty
      about the period of opportunity can erode the actual results. If the first mover mises-
      timates the timing of the window, a less attractive cash flow curve will result.
           An entrepreneur’s objective is to decide when to stop searching for additional
      information and enter the new market so as to maximize the expected profit. With
      insufficient information, a firm can enter too early and incur a large cost. How-
      ever, if it takes too long to gather sufficient information, the firm may lose the
      first-mover advantage. Entrepreneurs should stop searching for information and
      enter a market when they estimate that the marginal benefit of additional knowl-
      edge is less than the payoff of entry [Lévesque et al., 2009].
