     The difficulty with deciding whether to proceed to commercialize an inven-
tion can depend, especially, on the radicalness of the invention. Disruptive or
radical innovations introduce a set of attributes to a marketplace different from
the ones that mainstream customers historically have valued, and the products
often initially perform unfavorably along one or two dimensions of performance
that are particularly important to those customers. As a result, mainstream
customers are unwilling or unable to use disruptive products in applications
they know and understand. At first, therefore, disruptive innovations tend to
be used and valued only in new uncertain markets or applications.
