5.4 New Technology Ventures
Often, an entrepreneur encounters a new technology, but a compelling applica-
tion of the technology is not obvious. Thus, a new technology sometimes can be
characterized as a solution looking for a problem. Neither the first companies
to use the technology nor the companies with the best technology necessarily
win. Instead, the firms that find the right application for the technology succeed
[Balachandra et al., 2004].
     The elements of an attractive innovation strategy are provided in Table 5.4.
Any new venture should have a defined customer, one or two key benefits, a short
period to payback, and a proprietary advantage. Finally, the new venture team
must possess the necessary core competencies to exploit the new technology.
